<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auxerre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auxerre is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonne-river">Yonne</a>, prefecture of the Yonne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. The Roman Autessiodurum, it became an important bishopric associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanus-of-auxerre">Saint Germanus of Auxerre</a>, whose abbey survives. Its counts were long linked with Nevers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1435">Treaty of Arras</a> in 1435 gave the county to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> of Burgundy. It is known for its Gothic cathedral and the nearby Chablis vineyards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auxerre.jpg" alt="Auxerre" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auxerre_-_Yonne_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
